# English captions (seconds in the ORIGINAL recording NAF004.mp3). *word* = shown in gold.
# Cleaned by hand from TurboScribe (20:56-26:24 and 29:58-end re-transcribed from clips; Roman Urdu translated by hand).
CAPTIONS = [
    (0.10, 28.60, "Despite being at the feet of such a great personality, I have not taken full advantage of it. Even in reading and listening to his books and explaining them, the Arabic and Farsi in them, I don't have much command of that. But whatever I have heard and done, at least that much I can explain."),
    (28.60, 67.10, "A'udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajeem. Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem. Ya Qadir, Ya Sultan, Ya Haqq, Ya Rasul Allah ﷺ. We have been reading the book *Nizam-ul-Amal-e-Fuqara*."),
    (67.10, 87.90, 'Now we are going to read the topic on page 17. Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem.'),
    (114.60, 165.60, "“If he has faith and conviction, Allah can give without any means.” Allah fulfils the wishes of those who have faith. “When a person's thoughts are right and his belief in Allah is firm, and he obeys Him and follows His commands, then his *test* begins.”"),
    (173.30, 203.50, 'The great ones and the messengers are tested. Think of the test of Hazrat *Imam Hussain*, may Allah be pleased with him: a test so great that no one could bear it; only the great ones can. Their tests are great.'),
    (208.10, 274.50, '“Then the whole world becomes his enemy, and Allah puts him through trouble, *illness*, *poverty* and need.” So we should pray: may Allah save us from poverty and need, and from tests; may Allah help us beyond what we can do. “I must do this and that, and I can do nothing; I can get nothing”: He puts him in need.'),
    (274.50, 330.40, "“And Allah *reveals* to him: you must obey Me, not that I should obey you.” Allah has put everything in tests. Allah reveals, meaning He places it as a thought in his heart; Allah's command comes in the form of his thoughts, and he cannot avoid it. He knows it is His command. “The wife asks for a good life and good food…”"),
    (330.48, 345.00, 'Poverty on one side, illness on the other: this state is very hard to bear, and it needs a lot of *patience*.'),
    (348.18, 373.20, '“*Sabr* is to bear hardships and difficulties, even while the heart finds them hard.”'),
    (384.32, 390.38, "“Inna Allaha ma'as-sabireen”: surely Allah is with those who are *patient*."),
    (416.50, 448.10, "This is no ordinary thing. Look: the body's temperature should be *98.5*, no more and no less. If it goes above, it is fever; if it goes below, it is weakness."),
    (451.70, 505.40, "I don't need to say everything at once; I wait. Likewise, if the imam makes a slip in an ayah during namaz, don't correct him at once. Say “SubhanAllah”: you have made a mistake. Then he reads it again, once or twice. That is the rule. Too much is fever; too little is weakness."),
    (519.23, 537.37, '“There is only one *straight path*, and it is shorter than all the others.” People come from every direction; the paths are different; the straight path goes straight.'),
    (543.37, 555.59, '“Whoever is on the straight path reaches the destination *faster*.”'),
    (558.09, 566.53, 'What does it mean to be on the straight path? To *walk* on it.'),
    (571.12, 582.15, 'Whoever is on the straight path reaches the destination faster, because the path is *shorter*.'),
    (589.60, 595.50, '“*Innallaha yuhibbus-sabireen*”: surely Allah loves those who are patient.'),
    (599.29, 615.63, '“When a person bears difficulties and there is no *resentment* in his heart: no complaint, completely silent…”'),
    (617.65, 637.77, "“…and he accepts Allah's commands without any resentment, then it is called *Tasleem*.” This is the teaching of the elders."),
    (637.80, 716.40, 'Patience is a hard thing; but when he takes up difficulties *happily*, he has reached Tasleem. This is what the great ones wish for. Because Allah is *Qadir-e-Mutlaq*, the All-Powerful: He does what He wills. Our task is to be patient and to bear it.'),
    (716.40, 781.50, 'Do you know Farsi? “*Na mardiyo mardi qadame fasila darad*”: between unmanliness and manliness there is only one step. If you have a little courage, you will gain it. And one more thing: live with men of courage, and you will become one too.'),
    (781.50, 838.10, "If you have a little courage, if you try a little and don't give in to your heart, you will reach Tasleem. This is a great thing. “When a person becomes used to bearing pain, following Allah's commands becomes easy; and when he begins to find *sweetness* in every pain,” Allahu Akbar, that is better still."),
    (840.40, 888.80, '“Just as a person who eats bitter opium starts to enjoy it while eating it”: he bears its bitterness, and the intoxication that remains, the bitterness and the intoxication, both become one to him. He enjoys it.'),
    (896.14, 931.00, 'So its name is *Raza*. Pain keeps coming to him, but instead of crying out and complaining, he keeps praising Allah: SubhanAllah. He has reached Raza: *pleased*, happy however Allah keeps him.'),
    (939.50, 1034.40, "Allah's pleasure is a great thing. Someone said before Hazrat *Imam Hasan*, may Allah be pleased with him, that Hazrat *Abu Dharr al-Ghifari*, the great Companion, says: “I take pain with my right hand and comfort with my left.” Imam Hasan said: may Allah have mercy on Abu Dharr; I take both pain and comfort with my *right hand*. Allahu Akbar, what a station! This is Raza."),
    (1038.40, 1135.30, "“*Kilta yadayhi yamin*”: both of Allah's hands are right hands; whatever comes from Him is good. So far, whatever we have explained were *virtues*, the deeds that bring salvation. In contrast to them are the *vices*, the things that destroy."),
    (1135.30, 1193.40, 'The destructive things are those that ruin and cause loss; the vices are of a low level. The virtues were the means of salvation, salvation from the Fire. Some vices are of the *tongue*: lying, *gheebat* (backbiting), *chughal-khori* (carrying tales).'),
    (1194.50, 1271.40, 'And some are of the *heart*: *takabbur*, pride (“I am greater”); *ujb*, self-admiration (“I am good”); and *hasad*, envy: wanting to take away a blessing Allah has given to someone else. “Why did You give it to them? You should have given it to me.” He lives with a wish in his heart to harm them.'),
    (1271.40, 1301.00, 'Envy: “Allah has given them a blessing; take it away from them.” And *riya*: worshipping to show off. Some people worship to be seen; bad people do it, and we do it too. May Allah save us.'),
    (1301.00, 1348.90, 'And there are other things that even ordinary people know are bad: adultery, unnatural acts, killing, fleeing from battle, drinking and gambling. By doing all this a person suffers greatly; it is ruin.'),
    (1362.30, 1425.60, 'Remember well: before the *medicine* comes *abstinence*. First keep away from all these things. Save yourself from bad thoughts, and keep away from them. First give up the pleasure, then overcome it. When you leave pleasure, the door of *success* opens, and after that you progress.'),
    (1429.80, 1485.40, 'Then comes the *“I”*: I, I, I… “I am something special.” Now it has happened: now everyone is carefree. “I have passed the exam; now I am free.”'),
    (1487.20, 1509.90, "Don't think that once you have passed the exam you are free. No, no. The *exam* goes on; as long as you are alive, it stays strict. If you become careless, you will be humiliated."),
    (1540.50, 1584.30, "The nafs and *Shaytan*: beware of them, and of their work. If Allah saves you from these *two enemies*, you will be saved. So keep asking Allah's forgiveness, and keep praying to be saved."),
    (1584.32, 1600.50, 'What is all this? It lies *stiff* with the cold. All of this lies stiff because of the cold.'),
    (1600.50, 1607.44, 'But the moment a little *warmth* reaches it, it spreads its hood, rises up, and is ready to bite.'),
    (1607.44, 1621.40, 'To think it is *dead* is foolishness. It is inviting calamity. Foolishness!'),
    (1621.40, 1647.40, 'Shaytan is our *ancestral enemy*. We are the children of Adam, peace be upon him, and he had our forefather taken out of Jannah.'),
    (1647.40, 1677.62, 'He had him put out of the comfort of Jannah. Will he now let us into Jannah easily? *Never!*'),
    (1677.70, 1698.50, 'What an enemy he is: very cunning, very clever, and he cannot even be *seen*.'),
    (1698.50, 1723.96, "Wherever a person's blood flows, he can reach there. But when we recite *Qul a'udhu bi-rabbin-nas*, he runs away at once."),
    (1723.96, 1747.53, "So what will you do? Shaytan is the *dog* of the Divine court: it barks at the strangers it sees. Call out: *A'udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajeem!*"),
    (1747.53, 1776.39, 'Not just the words: say it with hatred for him and a longing to be saved. “I come into Your protection; save me from his evil.”'),
    (1776.39, 1789.06, "Call out A'udhu billah: the *Master* of this dog will call it back, and you will be safe from its harm."),
    (1789.10, 1863.70, "“When the nafs is refined to this degree, it is granted *fana-e-af'aal*, the effacement of one's own acts.” Alhamdulillah, what writing! See where he takes us. “Then he sees no benefit or harm coming from anyone but Allah.”"),
    (1865.60, 1928.50, "“In the valley of fana-e-af'aal, great claimants of fana in the attributes and the essence *stumble* and fall on their faces. Without refining the nafs, every claim of fana is empty: *words without meaning*.” “I am very refined, I am good, I am pure of all evil”: all of this is useless talk."),
    (1928.50, 1993.00, 'When our nafs is truly refined, then we progress. What is the real thing? *Fana* is the real thing. He has brought us to the last paragraph: doing all this, you are servants; enter into fana. Hold yourself to account, restrain the nafs, keep away from Shaytan, do good deeds, avoid evil.'),
    (2216.80, 2241.80, 'There is a whole book in this. When the *Divine Names* are reflected in the mirror of the heart, your form becomes exemplary, and then you attain *salvation*. SubhanAllah.'),
    (2299.30, 2325.20, 'Alhamdulillah, this is a lesson. The books I have read over the last two or three years, I am getting their *benefit*: what I learned, read and did is being *renewed* for me.'),
    (2328.80, 2341.80, 'Now, read the naat. Wasil Mian, Wasil Pasha: read the naat.'),
]
